This is a greedy assignment followed by a bounded local search.

**Greedy phase.** The simulation is event-driven. At each step, take the vehicle with the earliest free time. Among all unassigned rides that this vehicle can still finish on time, pick the one with the best score per unit of time used.

For a vehicle at position p and time t, and a ride i:
- arrival = t + dist(p, start_i)
- start = max(arrival, s_i)
- finish = start + d_i
- The ride is feasible only if finish ≤ f_i.
- score = d_i + (B if arrival ≤ s_i, else 0)
- cost = finish − t, which counts both the drive and any wait.

The ratio score/cost is a reasonable heuristic. A vehicle with no feasible ride is retired. Scanning every unassigned ride for each assignment costs O(N) per step, so the total is about N × N = 10^8 operations, which fits the time limit.

To implement this, I keep the vehicles in a priority queue keyed by free time. Each pop scans the list of unassigned rides. Rides that are no longer feasible for any vehicle are dropped. Since a vehicle's time only increases, a ride that is infeasible now stays infeasible for that vehicle, but it may still be feasible for another vehicle at an earlier position. The queue pops the earliest-free vehicle first, and later pops have larger times, so a ride that is infeasible at the current popped time stays infeasible for all later pops. Such rides can therefore be removed permanently, using the lower bound that the arrival time is at least t + dist and the finish is at least that plus d.

The cost can be zero only if the ride starts at the vehicle's position and the vehicle must wait, but the cost includes d ≥ 1, so it is always at least 1.

The score function is score/(cost) with a slight tweak: the cost is the full time taken, including waiting. I use doubles for the ratio.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main(){
    long long R,C,F,N,B,T;
    scanf("%lld %lld %lld %lld %lld %lld",&R,&C,&F,&N,&B,&T);
    vector<array<long long,6>> r(N);
    for(auto&q:r)for(int k=0;k<6;k++)scanf("%lld",&q[k]);
    vector<long long> px(F,0),py(F,0),tm(F,0);
    vector<vector<int>> res(F);
    priority_queue<pair<long long,int>,vector<pair<long long,int>>,greater<>> pq;
    for(int i=0;i<F;i++)pq.push({0,i});
    vector<int> rem(N);
    iota(rem.begin(),rem.end(),0);
    while(!pq.empty()){
        auto [t,v]=pq.top();pq.pop();
        double best=-1;int bi=-1;size_t bj=0;
        size_t w=0;
        for(size_t j=0;j<rem.size();j++){
            int i=rem[j];
            auto&q=r[i];
            long long d=llabs(q[2]-q[0])+llabs(q[3]-q[1]);
            long long arr=t+llabs(q[0]-px[v])+llabs(q[1]-py[v]);
            long long st=max(arr,q[4]);
            long long fin=st+d;
            if(t+d>q[5]) continue; // infeasible for all later pops
            rem[w++]=i;
            if(fin>q[5])continue;
            long long sc=d+(arr<=q[4]?B:0);
            double val=(double)sc/(double)(fin-t);
            if(val>best){best=val;bi=i;bj=w-1;}
        }
        rem.resize(w);
        if(bi<0)continue;
        auto&q=r[bi];
        long long d=llabs(q[2]-q[0])+llabs(q[3]-q[1]);
        long long arr=t+llabs(q[0]-px[v])+llabs(q[1]-py[v]);
        long long fin=max(arr,q[4])+d;
        res[v].push_back(bi);
        px[v]=q[2];py[v]=q[3];
        rem.erase(rem.begin()+bj);
        pq.push({fin,v});
    }
    for(int i=0;i<F;i++){
        printf("%d",(int)res[i].size());
        for(int x:res[i])printf(" %d",x);
        printf("\n");
    }
}
```

I have not compiled or run this against any data set.
